          "text": "     1→# CLAUDE.md\n     2→\n     3→This file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n     4→\n     5→## Project Overview\n     6→\n     7→`use-ai` is a TypeScript monorepo for building AI-powered React applications with Notion AI-like functionality. The library enables React components to expose tools (functions) to Claude AI via a WebSocket server, allowing users to control UI components through natural language prompts.\n     8→\n     9→**Key Innovation**: Tools are defined and executed on the client where application state lives, with the server acting as a coordinator between Claude API and client tools. This enables direct access to React state, browser APIs, and local data without backend dependencies.\n    10→\n    11→## Development Commands\n    12→\n    13→### Setup\n    14→```bash\n    15→bun install                    # Install dependencies\n    16→```\n    17→\n    18→### Development\n    19→```bash\n    20→export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...  # Required for server\n    21→bun run start:server           # Start AI server (port 8080)\n    22→bun run dev                    # Start example app with hot reload (port 3000)\n    23→```\n    24→\n    25→The server must be running before starting the example app.\n    26→\n    27→### Logging Configuration\n    28→```bash\n    29→export LOG_FORMAT=pretty       # Development: human-readable logs (default)\n    30→export LOG_FORMAT=json         # Production: structured JSON logs\n    31→```\n    32→\n    33→### Rate Limiting Configuration\n    34→```bash\n    35→export RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS=10      # Max requests per window (0 = unlimited)\n    36→export RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000      # Rate limit window in ms (default: 1 minute)\n    37→```\n    38→\n    39→### Building\n    40→```bash\n    41→bun run build                  # Build all packages (client, server, example)\n    42→bun run build:client           # Build @use-ai/client only\n    43→bun run build:server           # Build @use-ai/server only\n    44→```\n    45→\n    46→Build process: Bun bundles code → TypeScript generates type declarations → ESM output\n    47→\n    48→### Testing\n    49→```bash\n    50→bun test                       # Run all unit tests\n    51→bun run test:e2e               # Run E2E tests (requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)\n    52→bun run test:e2e:ui            # Interactive E2E test UI\n    53→bun run test:e2e:headed        # E2E tests with visible browser\n    54→bun run test:e2e:debug         # E2E tests in debug mode\n    55→```\n    56→\n    57→## Architecture\n    58→\n    59→### Monorepo Structure\n    60→- `packages/client/` - `@use-ai/client`: React hooks library for AI integrations\n    61→- `packages/server/` - `@use-ai/server`: WebSocket server for AI agent hosting\n    62→- `apps/example/` - Example AI-powered todo list demonstrating the library\n    63→\n    64→### Core Data Flow\n    65→\n    66→```\n    67→1. Tool Registration\n    68→   Client registers tools → Server stores tool definitions\n    69→\n    70→2. User Prompt + Component State\n    71→   User sends prompt → Client includes component state via `prompt` argument\n    72→   → Server forwards to Claude API with tool definitions\n    73→\n    74→3. AI Processing & Tool Selection\n    75→   Claude analyzes prompt + state → Returns tool_use blocks\n    76→\n    77→4. Tool Execution (Client-Side)\n    78→   Server sends tool_call to client → Client executes tool locally\n    79→   → Client waits for component re-render (to get updated state)\n    80→   → Client sends tool_response with new state back to server\n    81→\n    82→5. Final Response\n    83→   Server sends tool results to Claude → Claude generates response\n    84→   → Server streams response to client\n    85→```\n    86→\n    87→### Key Architectural Patterns\n    88→\n    89→#### Client-Side Tool Execution\n    90→- Tools are **defined and executed on the client** (where application state lives)\n    91→- Server acts as **coordinator** between Claude API and client tools\n    92→- This enables tools to access React state, browser APIs, and local data directly\n    93→- **Security benefit**: No backend API access means AI can only see/manipulate what's on the page\n    94→\n    95→#### Component State Management\n    96→- Component state is provided to AI via the `prompt` argument of `useAI` hook\n    97→- The AI only receives state from components using `useAI`, keeping context focused\n    98→- Library waits for component re-render before sending tool response to ensure latest state\n    99→- State is serialized to string (developer-controlled, not full HTML rendering)\n   100→\n   101→Example:\n   102→```typescript\n   103→useAI({\n   104→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   105→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`  // Current state AI sees\n   106→});\n   107→```\n   108→\n   109→#### Invisible Components\n   110→Components that provide tools but don't re-render (e.g., providers) use the `invisible` flag:\n   111→\n   112→```typescript\n   113→useAI({\n   114→  tools: { sendEmail },\n   115→  invisible: true  // Don't wait for re-render after tool calls\n   116→});\n   117→```\n   118→\n   119→Without this flag, the library would wait indefinitely for a re-render that never comes.\n   120→\n   121→#### Multiple Components of Same Type\n   122→Use `id` parameter to differentiate component instances:\n   123→\n   124→```typescript\n   125→function ListItem({ rowIndex }) {\n   126→  useAI({\n   127→    tools: { updateLabel, incrementCounter },\n   128→    id: `Row ${rowIndex}`  // Prefixes all tool names\n   129→  });\n   130→}\n   131→```\n   132→\n   133→Tools are registered as `Row 1/updateLabel`, `Row 2/updateLabel`, etc.\n   134→\n   135→If component has `id` attribute, it's automatically used:\n   136→```tsx\n   137→<ListItem id=\"Row 1\" />\n   138→```\n   139→\n   140→#### WebSocket Protocol\n   141→All messages follow `{ type: string, data: object }` format:\n   142→\n   143→**Client → Server:**\n   144→- `register_tools`: Send tool definitions (JSON Schema format)\n   145→- `prompt`: Send user prompt with optional conversation ID\n   146→- `tool_response`: Return tool execution result for a specific tool call\n   147→\n   148→**Server → Client:**\n   149→- `tool_call`: Request tool execution with `{ toolCallId, name, input }`\n   150→- `response`: Final AI response with conversation ID\n   151→- `error`: Error messages\n   152→\n   153→#### Promise-Based Tool Coordination\n   154→The server uses promises to synchronously wait for async tool execution across the WebSocket boundary:\n   155→\n   156→```typescript\n   157→// Server creates promise and stores resolver\n   158→const promise = new Promise(resolve => {\n   159→  session.pendingToolCalls.set(toolCallId, resolve);\n   160→});\n   161→\n   162→// Send tool_call to client\n   163→this.sendMessage(ws, 'tool_call', { toolCallId, name, input });\n   164→\n   165→// Wait for client to execute and send tool_response\n   166→const result = await promise;\n   167→```\n   168→\n   169→This enables synchronous-style code despite async WebSocket communication.\n   170→\n   171→#### Conversation History Management\n   172→- Server maintains **full conversation history per WebSocket session**\n   173→- History includes user messages, assistant messages (with tool_use blocks), and tool results\n   174→- Enables multi-turn conversations with context preservation\n   175→- History is lost on disconnect (session-based, not persisted to database)\n   176→- **Prompt caching**: Anthropic prompt caching automatically used for repeated conversations\n   177→\n   178→#### Tool Aggregation Pattern\n   179→- `UseAIProvider` maintains a registry of tools from all mounted `useAI` hooks\n   180→- Tools from multiple components are merged into a single set\n   181→- Each hook gets a unique ID for registration/cleanup\n   182→- Enables compositional tool registration across component tree\n   183→- Single chat interface knows about all tools currently rendered on page\n   184→\n   185→### Tool Definition System\n   186→\n   187→Tools are defined using `defineTool()` with Zod schemas:\n   188→\n   189→```typescript\n   190→import { defineTool } from '@use-ai/client';\n   191→import { z } from 'zod';\n   192→\n   193→// With arguments\n   194→const addTodo = defineTool(\n   195→  'Add a new todo item to the list',\n   196→  z.object({ text: z.string() }),\n   197→  (input) => {\n   198→    // Implementation with typed input\n   199→    return { success: true, message: 'Todo added' };\n   200→  }\n   201→);\n   202→\n   203→// Without arguments\n   204→const logout = defineTool(\n   205→  'Log the current user out',\n   206→  () => { /* logout logic */ }\n   207→);\n   208→\n   209→// With confirmation required (dangerous actions)\n   210→const deleteAccount = defineTool(\n   211→  'Delete this user\\'s account permanently',\n   212→  () => { /* deletion logic */ },\n   213→  { confirmationRequired: true }\n   214→);\n   215→```\n   216→\n   217→**Under the hood:**\n   218→- Zod schema is converted to JSON Schema (via `zod-to-json-schema`) for Claude API\n   219→- Original Zod schema is retained for runtime validation\n   220→- `_execute()` method validates input with Zod before calling function\n   221→- TypeScript types are inferred from Zod schema\n   222→- `confirmationRequired` option aggressively prompts AI to ask for confirmation (not 100% foolproof)\n   223→\n   224→### Multi-Tool Use Support\n   225→\n   226→The library supports Claude's multi-tool use feature, enabling emergent \"bulk action\" behavior:\n   227→\n   228→```typescript\n   229→useAI({\n   230→  tools: { addTodo, deleteTodo },\n   231→  prompt: `Todo List: ${JSON.stringify(todos)}`\n   232→});\n   233→```\n   234→\n   235→User: \"add a shopping list to make tonkotsu ramen\"\n   236→\n   237→Claude will automatically call `addTodo` multiple times for each ingredient, even though only single-item operations are defined. This is a powerful emergent behavior.\n   238→\n   239→### State Synchronization Pattern\n   240→\n   241→The example app demonstrates a pub/sub pattern for keeping UI in sync when tools mutate state:\n   242→\n   243→```typescript\n   244→const listeners = new Set<() => void>();\n   245→\n   246→export function subscribeTodos(listener: () => void) {\n   247→  listeners.add(listener);\n   248→  return () => listeners.delete(listener);\n   249→}\n   250→\n   251→function notifyListeners() {\n   252→  listeners.forEach(fn => fn());\n   253→}\n   254→\n   255→// Tools call notifyListeners() after state mutations\n   256→```\n   257→\n   258→This enables both AI operations and manual UI operations to update the same state.\n   259→\n   260→### Custom UI Support\n   261→\n   262→Default chat UI can be replaced with custom components:\n   263→\n   264→```typescript\n   265→<UseAIProvider\n   266→  serverUrl=\"ws://localhost:8080\"\n   267→  CustomButton={MyCustomButton}  // Replace floating button\n   268→  CustomChat={MyCustomChat}      // Replace chat interface\n   269→>\n   270→  <App />\n   271→</UseAIProvider>\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→## Security\n   275→\n   276→### Rate Limiting\n   277→- Configured via `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` and `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` env vars\n   278→- Per-connection rate limits prevent abuse of LLM credits\n   279→- Each WebSocket connection is rate-limited independently\n   280→\n   281→### \"Lethal Trifecta\" Mitigations\n   282→Following [Simon Willison's security guidance](https://simonwillison.net/2025/Jun/16/the-lethal-trifecta/):\n   283→\n   284→1. **Limited internet access**: Server cannot invoke tools to access external internet (no curl, search, etc.)\n   285→2. **No backend API access**: V1 cannot access backend APIs directly\n   286→3. **Client-only scope**: AI can only see what's on the page and take actions users can already perform\n   287→\n   288→This prevents data exfiltration and limits attack surface.\n   289→\n   290→### Dangerous Actions\n   291→Tools can use `confirmationRequired: true` option to aggressively prompt AI for user confirmation before execution. This is not 100% foolproof as AI may ignore prompts.\n   292→\n   293→### No Backend API Access (V1)\n   294→Current version intentionally does not provide backend API integration to minimize security risks and complexity. All tools must be implemented in frontend code.\n   295→\n   296→## TypeScript Configuration\n   297→\n   298→- **Strict mode enabled**: All type checks enforced\n   299→- **Module system**: ESM throughout (`\"type\": \"module\"` in all package.json files)\n   300→- **Module resolution**: `\"bundler\"` (Bun-specific)\n   301→- **Target**: ES2022\n   302→- **Never use `any` type unless unavoidable**\n   303→\n   304→## Dependencies\n   305→\n   306→### Client Package\n   307→- `zod` (v4.x): Schema validation and JSON Schema generation\n   308→- `zod-to-json-schema`: Converts Zod schemas to JSON Schema for Claude API\n   309→- `react` (peer dependency): ^18.0.0\n   310→\n   311→### Server Package\n   312→- `@anthropic-ai/sdk`: Claude API integration\n   313→- `ws`: WebSocket server implementation\n   314→\n   315→## Important Implementation Details\n   316→\n   317→### Connection Management\n   318→- Client auto-reconnects on disconnect (5 attempts with exponential backoff)\n   319→- Each `useAI` hook creates a dedicated WebSocket client instance\n   320→- Provider monitors connection status for UI feedback\n   321→\n   322→### Error Handling\n   323→- Tool execution errors are caught and sent back to Claude as `tool_result` content\n   324→- Zod validation errors propagate as tool execution errors\n   325→- Server errors sent as `error` messages to client\n   326→- Client handles errors via optional `onError` callback in `useAI` hook\n   327→\n   328→### Build System\n   329→- Bun is used for both runtime and bundling\n   330→- Package builds: `bun build` creates bundle → `tsc --emitDeclarationOnly` generates types\n   331→- Example app build: `bun build --target browser --minify`\n   332→- All packages output ESM modules\n   333→\n   334→### Environment Variables\n   335→\n   336→**Server:**\n   337→- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (required): Claude API key\n   338→- `LOG_FORMAT` (optional): `pretty` (default) or `json`\n   339→- `RATE_LIMIT_MAX_REQUESTS` (optional): Max requests per window (0 = unlimited, default: 0)\n   340→- `RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS` (optional): Rate limit window in milliseconds (default: 60000)\n   341→\n   342→**Port Configuration:**\n   343→- Server runs on port 8080 (hardcoded in `packages/server/src/start.ts`)\n   344→- Example app runs on port 3000 (configured in `apps/example/package.json`)\n   345→\n   346→### File Naming Conventions\n   347→- React components: PascalCase (e.g., `UseAIFloatingButton.tsx`)\n   348→- Utilities and hooks: camelCase (e.g., `defineTool.ts`, `useAI.ts`)\n   349→- Main entry points: `index.ts` or `index.tsx`\n   350→\n   351→## Testing Strategy\n   352→\n   353→### Unit Tests\n   354→- Located alongside source files (e.g., `defineTool.test.ts`)\n   355→- Run with `bun test`\n   356→- Test individual functions and utilities\n   357→\n   358→### E2E Tests\n   359→- Located in `apps/example/test/`\n   360→- Use Playwright with real Claude API calls\n   361→- Test actual AI interactions including edge cases\n   362→- Require `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n   363→- Run with `bun run test:e2e`\n   364→\n   365→E2E tests are preferred for AI features because the AI can be unpredictable and we want to catch edge cases with real API interactions.\n   366→\n   367→## Deployment\n   368→\n   369→The server is designed to be deployed as a separate service on Kubernetes:\n   370→\n   371→- Has in-memory state only (active WebSocket sessions)\n   372→- Can be horizontally scaled\n   373→- Shared across multiple applications (ProOne, MeetsMore, etc.)\n   374→- Requires only `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable\n   375→- Different products can share same server instance (JWT signing keys may differ)\n   376→\n   377→## Common Development Tasks\n   378→\n   379→### Adding a New Tool\n   380→1. Define tool with `defineTool()` in component\n   381→2. Add tool to `useAI` hook's `tools` object\n   382→3. Update component state in `prompt` argument\n   383→4. Write unit test for tool function\n   384→5. Write E2E test for AI interaction\n   385→\n   386→### Adding a New Feature\n   387→1. Plan implementation (consider client vs server changes)\n   388→2. Update TypeScript types as needed\n   389→3. Implement client-side changes first\n   390→4. Update server if needed (rare for V1)\n   391→5. Add tests (unit + E2E)\n   392→6. Build packages: `bun run build:client` and/or `bun run build:server`\n   393→7. Verify in example app\n   394→\n   395→### Debugging WebSocket Issues\n   396→1. Check server logs (use `LOG_FORMAT=pretty` for readability)\n   397→2. Check browser console for client-side errors\n   398→3. Verify WebSocket connection in browser DevTools Network tab\n   399→4. Check conversation history in server logs\n   400→5. Verify tool registration messages\n   401→\n   402→### Debugging AI Behavior\n   403→1. Check the `prompt` being sent (component state)\n   404→2. Verify tool descriptions are clear and accurate\n   405→3. Check if multi-tool use is causing unexpected behavior\n   406→4. Review conversation history in server logs\n   407→5. Test with E2E tests to reproduce consistently\n   408→\n   409→## Architecture Decisions\n   410→\n   411→### Why Client-Side Tool Execution?\n   412→- Application state lives in React components\n   413→- No need for state synchronization between frontend and backend\n   414→- Direct access to browser APIs and local data\n   415→- Simpler architecture with fewer moving parts\n   416→- Better security (no backend API access in V1)\n   417→\n   418→### Why WebSocket Instead of HTTP?\n   419→- Real-time bidirectional communication needed for tool calls\n   420→- Conversation history maintained per session\n   421→- Lower latency for interactive chat experience\n   422→- Easier to implement streaming responses\n   423→\n   424→### Why No Backend API Access in V1?\n   425→- Reduces security risks (limits \"Lethal Trifecta\")\n   426→- Simpler architecture and deployment\n   427→- Forces developers to think about what AI can access\n   428→- Sufficient for many use cases (UI manipulation, client state)\n   429→\n   430→### Why Zod for Tool Definitions?\n   431→- Type safety at compile time\n   432→- Runtime validation of LLM tool calls\n   433→- Automatic JSON Schema generation for Claude API\n   434→- Great developer experience with TypeScript integration\n   435→\n   436→## Code Organization\n   437→\n   438→- `packages/client/src/components/` - React UI components (chat, button)\n   439→- `packages/client/src/hooks/` - React hooks (`useAI`, `useAIContext`)\n   440→- `packages/client/src/utils/` - Utilities (`defineTool`, WebSocket client)\n   441→- `packages/server/src/` - Server implementation (`UseAIServer` class)\n   442→- `apps/example/src/` - Example app demonstrating features\n   443→- `apps/example/test/` - E2E tests with Playwright\n   444→\n   445→## Future Considerations\n   446→\n   447→### Potential V2 Features (Not Yet Implemented)\n   448→- Backend API integration with authentication/authorization\n   449→- JWT-based user authentication for rate limiting\n   450→- Persistent conversation history (database storage)\n   451→- Tool execution on server side (for backend operations)\n   452→- Support for multiple LLM providers (OpenAI, etc.)\n   453→- Streaming tool execution results\n   454→- Tool composition and chaining\n   455→\n   456→### Known Limitations\n   457→- Conversation history is session-based (lost on disconnect)\n   458→- No authentication/authorization built-in (V1)\n   459→- Rate limiting is per-connection, not per-user\n   460→- `confirmationRequired` is not foolproof (AI may ignore)\n   461→- No support for backend tool execution (V1)\n   462→- Single WebSocket server instance (no clustering with shared state)\n   463→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
